WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Before you touch a single tool, you need to understand what HVAC systems
actually do.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Every HVAC system has the same basic job: move heat from where you do not want
it to where you do. In cooling mode, the system moves heat from inside the
building to outside. In heating mode, it moves heat from a fuel source (gas,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Here are the key terms. Write these down.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Split System: An HVAC system with components split between outdoor (condenser)
and indoor (air handler/furnace) units, connected by refrigerant lines.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Condenser: The outdoor unit containing the compressor, condenser coil, and fan
motor. Rejects heat to the outdoor air.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Evaporator: The indoor coil where cold refrigerant absorbs heat from room air.
Located above the furnace or inside the air handler.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Here's why this matters on the job: On every service call, you must quickly
identify all system components and understand how they connect. A technician who
cannot trace the refrigerant circuit or airflow path cannot diagnose problems.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Watch out for these: The suction line is ALWAYS the larger of the two
refrigerant lines — it carries low-pressure vapor. And — Never confuse the
supply duct (conditioned air out) with the return duct (room air back)

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
